WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.689 --> 00:00:01.522
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:01.522 --> 00:00:02.355
Sal Khan here.

00:00:02.355 --> 00:00:04.499
Welcome to the homeroom livestream.

00:00:04.499 --> 00:00:07.569
We have a really exciting
guest today, Pedro Noguera,

00:00:07.569 --> 00:00:10.919
who is the Dean of the
Rossier School of Education

00:00:10.919 --> 00:00:13.119
at University of Southern California.

00:00:13.119 --> 00:00:15.359
So start thinking of your questions,

00:00:15.359 --> 00:00:17.939
putting them on the message
board on YouTube or Facebook,

00:00:17.939 --> 00:00:19.229
wherever you're watching this.

00:00:19.229 --> 00:00:21.889
And we're gonna try to get
as many questions as possible

00:00:21.889 --> 00:00:24.449
to Dean Noguera about
all things education,

00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:26.819
higher education, what's going on in K-12,

00:00:26.819 --> 00:00:28.209
I think all of that is fair game.

00:00:28.209 --> 00:00:30.529
Whatever is of interest to you.

00:00:30.529 --> 00:00:32.159
But before we jump into that conversation,

00:00:32.159 --> 00:00:34.359
I'll give my standard announcements.

00:00:34.359 --> 00:00:36.359
First of all, a reminder that Khan Academy

00:00:36.359 --> 00:00:38.109
is a not-for-profit organization.

00:00:38.109 --> 00:00:41.389
We only exist through
philanthropic donations

00:00:41.389 --> 00:00:43.019
from folks like yourself.

00:00:43.019 --> 00:00:44.399
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:44.399 --> 00:00:45.879
please think about making a donation

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:49.459
at khanacademy.org/donate.

00:00:49.459 --> 00:00:51.629
Also want to give a special
shout out to several

00:00:51.629 --> 00:00:55.079
organizations that
stepped up when COVID hit.

00:00:55.079 --> 00:00:57.029
And we saw that our costs had gone up

00:00:57.029 --> 00:00:59.719
because we were seeing
250% of normal usage,

00:00:59.719 --> 00:01:02.749
and we were trying to accelerate
a whole series of programs.

00:01:02.749 --> 00:01:05.059
So special, thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:01:05.059 --> 00:01:07.009
AT&amp;T, fastly, Novartis,

00:01:07.009 --> 00:01:08.929
and the many other funders at all levels,

00:01:08.929 --> 00:01:10.439
including many of y'all,

00:01:10.439 --> 00:01:12.879
for helping keep Khan Academy going.

00:01:12.879 --> 00:01:14.179
We continue to need your help.

00:01:14.179 --> 00:01:15.849
So once again, if you're
in a position to do so,

00:01:15.849 --> 00:01:17.769
please think about making a donation.

00:01:17.769 --> 00:01:20.989
Last but not least,
reminder for everyone about

00:01:20.989 --> 00:01:23.409
Homeroom with Sal, the podcast,

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:25.539
wherever you can find your podcast.

00:01:25.539 --> 00:01:28.109
It's an audio version of
some of the highlights

00:01:28.109 --> 00:01:31.009
from this livestream that you can consume

00:01:31.009 --> 00:01:32.259
safely in your car.

00:01:32.259 --> 00:01:34.729
So encourage you to check that out.

00:01:34.729 --> 00:01:35.562
So with that,

00:01:35.562 --> 00:01:38.209
I'm excited to introduce Pedro Noguera,

00:01:38.209 --> 00:01:41.699
Dean of the Rossier School
of Education at USC.

00:01:41.699 --> 00:01:44.079
Pedro, thanks for joining us.

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:47.269
- Thanks for having me,
Sal, great to be with you.

00:01:47.269 --> 00:01:49.669
- So there is a ton to talk about,

00:01:49.669 --> 00:01:51.399
maybe the best place to start.

00:01:51.399 --> 00:01:54.449
I mean, I wanna talk about given
you're in higher education,

00:01:54.449 --> 00:01:56.959
but you're in higher education
at the school of education,

00:01:56.959 --> 00:01:58.329
I think you have a really interesting lens

00:01:58.329 --> 00:02:01.079
on both higher education and K-12.

00:02:01.079 --> 00:02:03.699
Big picture, where do you think we are

00:02:03.699 --> 00:02:05.752
on the COVID crisis and education?

00:02:07.359 --> 00:02:09.089
What's gonna be the main damage done

00:02:09.089 --> 00:02:10.839
and what are the big opportunities?

00:02:12.179 --> 00:02:14.259
- Wow, so, so much damage.

00:02:14.259 --> 00:02:18.999
We just released a report today
from USC showing the impact

00:02:18.999 --> 00:02:21.799
of virtual learning on
kids, just in the LA area.

00:02:21.799 --> 00:02:25.789
And there are still
thousands of kids who have

00:02:25.789 --> 00:02:30.059
uneven or real total lack of
access to virtual learning.

00:02:30.059 --> 00:02:31.439
And that's not been corrected.

00:02:31.439 --> 00:02:34.159
And I'm not faulting the
district 'cause they did

00:02:34.159 --> 00:02:37.159
and have gone to great lengths
to try to get screens to kids

00:02:37.159 --> 00:02:38.789
and to connect it to the internet.

00:02:38.789 --> 00:02:42.569
But we still have lots of
communities across the country,

00:02:42.569 --> 00:02:45.219
particularly in rural areas,
but urban areas as well,

00:02:45.219 --> 00:02:47.829
where there's no internet service.

00:02:47.829 --> 00:02:50.782
So it starts there, just no access.

00:02:51.879 --> 00:02:54.469
Then we have the fact that
there are, as we know,

00:02:54.469 --> 00:02:57.669
many affluent people are
able to put their kids

00:02:57.669 --> 00:03:01.109
into learning pods, they
get hired private tutors,

00:03:01.109 --> 00:03:04.189
there're independent schools
where kids are coming back.

00:03:04.189 --> 00:03:06.009
That's not happening for
kids who are dependent on

00:03:06.009 --> 00:03:09.419
the public schools, especially
in our low income districts.

00:03:09.419 --> 00:03:14.419
So the disparities that were
there before the pandemic,

00:03:14.569 --> 00:03:16.212
are gonna be worse after.

00:03:17.729 --> 00:03:19.699
- And I guess, two questions.

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:22.099
Is there anything we can do to help

00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:23.449
mitigate some of that damage?

00:03:23.449 --> 00:03:25.079
Or if we're not, how do we deal with it

00:03:25.079 --> 00:03:27.892
once we do normalize,
whatever that might be?

00:03:29.099 --> 00:03:33.849
- Well, I start by just
reminding the viewers,

00:03:33.849 --> 00:03:36.329
all along several of us have been saying,

00:03:36.329 --> 00:03:38.529
we have fixated on the wrong thing.

00:03:38.529 --> 00:03:40.969
We were calling this an achievement gap,

00:03:40.969 --> 00:03:42.869
when we should have been always framing it

00:03:42.869 --> 00:03:44.649
as an opportunity gap.

00:03:44.649 --> 00:03:48.019
That is that kids need
the opportunity to learn.

00:03:48.019 --> 00:03:50.829
And that means once you frame it that way,

00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:52.559
then you start to look at the conditions

00:03:52.559 --> 00:03:54.739
under which teaching and learning occur.

00:03:54.739 --> 00:03:56.659
Those conditions have been very uneven,

00:03:56.659 --> 00:03:59.239
and all our fixation on test scores

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:00.399
has never addressed that.

00:04:00.399 --> 00:04:02.029
We never have made sure, for example,

00:04:02.029 --> 00:04:04.019
that kids have access to capable teachers,

00:04:04.019 --> 00:04:06.234
that they're in schools
with lab equipment,

00:04:06.234 --> 00:04:10.539
and libraries, basic
things that kids need.

00:04:10.539 --> 00:04:14.289
And now we have the issues of
access around online learning.

00:04:14.289 --> 00:04:17.489
And again, if you're an affluent
kid and something is hard,

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:21.469
you can get a private tutor,
you can go onto Khan Academy.

00:04:21.469 --> 00:04:23.609
Now, hopefully Khan Academy
is available to everybody,

00:04:23.609 --> 00:04:26.589
but there are a lot of kids
who don't have those resources.

00:04:26.589 --> 00:04:30.889
And so my hope is that
policymakers will become much more

00:04:30.889 --> 00:04:34.009
attentive to these gaps in opportunity

00:04:34.009 --> 00:04:36.359
that are pervasive
throughout American society.

00:04:37.359 --> 00:04:39.529
- Speaking about the opportunity
gap, I couldn't agree more.

00:04:39.529 --> 00:04:42.419
I mean, the digital divide
is the most obvious one,

00:04:42.419 --> 00:04:44.659
especially during COVID,
when you need that

00:04:44.659 --> 00:04:46.859
to just even be able to
access your potential.

00:04:46.859 --> 00:04:48.422
But even outside of COVID,

00:04:48.422 --> 00:04:50.929
if you have even a handful
of kids in the classroom

00:04:50.929 --> 00:04:52.439
who don't have internet access at home

00:04:52.439 --> 00:04:53.939
or proper internet access,

00:04:53.939 --> 00:04:56.006
the whole classroom's
not going to be able to

00:04:56.006 --> 00:04:58.969
take advantage of
personalized learning tools

00:04:58.969 --> 00:05:00.369
or whatever else.

00:05:00.369 --> 00:05:02.639
And then I was surprised, I
remember several months ago

00:05:02.639 --> 00:05:05.779
reading a report, that a
majority of minority majority

00:05:05.779 --> 00:05:07.449
schools don't even offer, high schools,

00:05:07.449 --> 00:05:09.579
don't offer a lot of the
courses that we take for granted

00:05:09.579 --> 00:05:10.419
in a high school.

00:05:10.419 --> 00:05:11.669
And when they do offer it,

00:05:11.669 --> 00:05:13.969
they don't cover it at
the same level of rigor

00:05:13.969 --> 00:05:16.119
'cause their expectations, arguably

00:05:16.119 --> 00:05:17.669
whatever something's going on,

00:05:17.669 --> 00:05:19.499
they might not be having
the same level of equipment,

00:05:19.499 --> 00:05:22.579
they might not be having
the same level of expertise

00:05:22.579 --> 00:05:24.889
amongst some of the
faculty, we don't know.

00:05:24.889 --> 00:05:26.729
But these kids aren't
getting the opportunity

00:05:26.729 --> 00:05:28.759
to even engage at that level.

00:05:28.759 --> 00:05:31.469
What do you see as the major levers?

00:05:31.469 --> 00:05:33.539
If you were secretary of education

00:05:33.539 --> 00:05:34.969
or emperor of the universe,

00:05:34.969 --> 00:05:37.442
what would you do to help close that?

00:05:38.819 --> 00:05:41.099
- Well, I think it has to go
beyond secretary of education.

00:05:41.099 --> 00:05:44.399
I think the federal
government, state governments,

00:05:44.399 --> 00:05:47.409
should be leaning hard on
the high tech companies.

00:05:47.409 --> 00:05:50.199
These companies are the
richest companies in the world

00:05:50.199 --> 00:05:54.019
right now, and have gotten
richer even during the pandemic.

00:05:54.019 --> 00:05:56.489
You think about Amazon, Facebook, Google,

00:05:56.489 --> 00:05:59.819
these companies are
massive, they're large.

00:05:59.819 --> 00:06:02.809
They have a social
responsibility to invest in

00:06:02.809 --> 00:06:06.069
the infrastructure that allows access.

00:06:06.069 --> 00:06:09.019
If you think about it, telephone service

00:06:09.019 --> 00:06:10.959
would not be available in rural areas,

00:06:10.959 --> 00:06:12.639
if we just saw it as a cost.

00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:15.439
We recognized that there's a basic need

00:06:15.439 --> 00:06:17.219
and right to communication.

00:06:17.219 --> 00:06:19.209
And so therefore, we do have cell towers

00:06:19.209 --> 00:06:23.139
and telephone service, even in
the most remote rural areas.

00:06:23.139 --> 00:06:27.001
We need that kind of
a commitment to access

00:06:27.001 --> 00:06:30.179
to basic services around the country.

00:06:30.179 --> 00:06:32.889
I think the federal
government has to lean in,

00:06:32.889 --> 00:06:34.589
and we might need legislation,

00:06:34.589 --> 00:06:38.369
but we've got to figure out
a way to make that possible,

00:06:38.369 --> 00:06:42.129
because not only is it about
learning opportunities,

00:06:42.129 --> 00:06:45.019
but as we know, we rely on the
internet for everything now.

00:06:45.019 --> 00:06:49.259
And to see so many people
cut off is I think again,

00:06:49.259 --> 00:06:52.669
a reflection of the deep
and persistent inequality

00:06:52.669 --> 00:06:53.502
in our country.

00:06:54.649 --> 00:06:56.129
- No, you're absolutely right.

00:06:56.129 --> 00:06:59.519
In all of the opportunity
gaps that need to be filled,

00:06:59.519 --> 00:07:01.999
I mean, the most obvious
one especially during COVID,

00:07:01.999 --> 00:07:04.089
is clearly the digital divide.

00:07:04.089 --> 00:07:06.163
And I mean, any sense,
we're signatories to this

00:07:06.163 --> 00:07:07.959
Connect All Students campaign,

00:07:07.959 --> 00:07:09.729
that Common Sense Media is doing.

00:07:09.729 --> 00:07:12.449
And you know, my back of the
envelope calculations are

00:07:12.449 --> 00:07:14.599
you could connect all the
families that aren't connected,

00:07:14.599 --> 00:07:15.829
in the whole country,

00:07:15.829 --> 00:07:19.849
for 1% of one of the rounds
of stimulus that were.

00:07:19.849 --> 00:07:24.169
So it feels like the most
obvious thing in the world to do.

00:07:24.169 --> 00:07:25.709
And it economically empowers people,

00:07:25.709 --> 00:07:27.379
probably even improves
their health outcomes

00:07:27.379 --> 00:07:31.719
'cause they'll get access
to information and whatnot.

00:07:31.719 --> 00:07:36.389
What's your view from how,

00:07:36.389 --> 00:07:38.259
what are teachers seeing and feeling,

00:07:38.259 --> 00:07:40.859
and how might, do you think
what we're seeing in COVID

00:07:40.859 --> 00:07:42.179
is it's just a COVID thing?

00:07:42.179 --> 00:07:45.119
Or do you think there's
certain aspects of teaching

00:07:45.119 --> 00:07:46.999
or schooling that might be forever altered

00:07:46.999 --> 00:07:48.799
because of what we're going through?

00:07:50.729 --> 00:07:53.069
- Well I think, I was
just on a call yesterday

00:07:53.069 --> 00:07:55.299
with some teachers in Texas, San Antonio.

00:07:55.299 --> 00:07:58.319
And many of them, their primary concern

00:07:58.319 --> 00:08:01.479
was the mental health needs of kids,

00:08:01.479 --> 00:08:03.789
and the stress that they're
seeing their children under.

00:08:03.789 --> 00:08:06.129
Kids who are not going outside,

00:08:06.129 --> 00:08:10.239
kids who are depressed and anxious

00:08:10.239 --> 00:08:12.759
because of the climate in the country.

00:08:12.759 --> 00:08:16.239
And I'm worried about the longterm effects

00:08:16.239 --> 00:08:18.259
and consequences of that.

00:08:18.259 --> 00:08:22.499
You also have the same
kinds of stress on teachers.

00:08:22.499 --> 00:08:25.419
Just think about the teachers
who are juggling kids at home,

00:08:25.419 --> 00:08:28.719
while they're expected
to do Zoom instruction.

00:08:28.719 --> 00:08:32.809
So unfortunately when you're
preoccupied with those things,

00:08:32.809 --> 00:08:36.039
how do you get creative
on the use of Zoom,

00:08:36.039 --> 00:08:38.999
and online platforms to provide

00:08:38.999 --> 00:08:40.989
really high quality instruction?

00:08:40.989 --> 00:08:42.889
There are some schools
that are doing that,

00:08:42.889 --> 00:08:45.569
they provided the support
and guidance to teachers,

00:08:45.569 --> 00:08:48.649
but I think that that's not
happening nearly enough.

00:08:48.649 --> 00:08:52.379
And so what we're seeing
as many kids who get,

00:08:52.379 --> 00:08:55.189
they're discouraged,
they're not motivated,

00:08:55.189 --> 00:08:58.909
they're burnt out from
too many hours online,

00:08:58.909 --> 00:09:00.199
and they're giving up.

00:09:00.199 --> 00:09:02.829
And that worries me, especially
for the older students

00:09:02.829 --> 00:09:05.959
who should be preparing
for college right now.

00:09:05.959 --> 00:09:07.809
- No, that makes a ton of sense.

00:09:07.809 --> 00:09:09.389
I mean, what if you were able,

00:09:09.389 --> 00:09:11.395
we have a lot of teachers who watch this.

00:09:11.395 --> 00:09:13.879
What would you tell them?

00:09:13.879 --> 00:09:15.489
I mean, as you just described,

00:09:15.489 --> 00:09:17.029
there's a lot of stressors going on,

00:09:17.029 --> 00:09:19.497
and they're trying to,
they're doing heroic efforts.

00:09:19.497 --> 00:09:21.649
What advice would you have for them

00:09:21.649 --> 00:09:23.892
to kinda navigate all of this?

00:09:24.879 --> 00:09:28.462
- I would strongly encourage
them to go onto Edutopia.

00:09:29.419 --> 00:09:32.839
That's my favorite platform
because they provide

00:09:32.839 --> 00:09:36.899
very practical guidance to
teachers, on first of all,

00:09:36.899 --> 00:09:40.049
how do you provide social
and emotional support to kids

00:09:40.049 --> 00:09:41.599
in an online medium?

00:09:41.599 --> 00:09:46.149
How do you design good
lessons K through 12,

00:09:46.149 --> 00:09:48.209
in a variety of subjects for kids,

00:09:48.209 --> 00:09:51.729
that can get kids engaged
and help kids to develop

00:09:51.729 --> 00:09:55.899
their skills and ensure
that learning is occurring?

00:09:55.899 --> 00:09:59.679
So you need those kinds of hands-on tools

00:09:59.679 --> 00:10:00.969
available for teachers.

00:10:00.969 --> 00:10:03.979
Many districts don't have
the resources to provide it.

00:10:03.979 --> 00:10:06.679
So unfortunately, it falls
on the back of teachers

00:10:06.679 --> 00:10:08.959
to figure out where can
I get the help I need?

00:10:08.959 --> 00:10:10.579
But there's help available there.

00:10:10.579 --> 00:10:13.549
And I'd really encourage
you to go check it out

00:10:13.549 --> 00:10:16.309
if you're a teacher out
there listening, Edutopia.

00:10:16.309 --> 00:10:17.269
I don't get paid.

00:10:17.269 --> 00:10:19.979
So this is a free commercial.

00:10:19.979 --> 00:10:20.812
I just think it's great.

00:10:20.812 --> 00:10:22.339
- No, they're a not-for-profit,

00:10:22.339 --> 00:10:24.266
that's George Lucas's effort.

00:10:25.109 --> 00:10:27.969
And I'm also a very big fan of them.

00:10:27.969 --> 00:10:30.252
So we're aligned there.

00:10:31.609 --> 00:10:35.059
Are there going to be some fallout?

00:10:35.059 --> 00:10:38.389
I mean, we've talked about
some of the negatives

00:10:38.389 --> 00:10:39.779
that are gonna happen over,

00:10:39.779 --> 00:10:41.669
I mean, frankly were
already happening pre-COVID,

00:10:41.669 --> 00:10:43.949
and if anything, COVID's
making it worse around

00:10:43.949 --> 00:10:45.932
opportunity gaps and digital divide.

00:10:47.319 --> 00:10:49.459
Do you think that people have,

00:10:49.459 --> 00:10:51.929
obviously your traditional
standardized state assessments

00:10:51.929 --> 00:10:54.260
have had to go on hold because of COVID,

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:56.169
every teacher I talk to,
they're thinking about

00:10:56.169 --> 00:10:58.899
how do they use Zoom in
a more interactive way.

00:10:58.899 --> 00:11:02.039
People are getting creative
in terms of breakout sessions,

00:11:02.039 --> 00:11:04.139
use of things like Khan Academy.

00:11:04.139 --> 00:11:06.809
Do you think that some
of this is going to,

00:11:06.809 --> 00:11:08.997
people are right now are
doing hybrids, where even if,

00:11:08.997 --> 00:11:10.679
when they are coming back to school,

00:11:10.679 --> 00:11:12.139
there's gonna be some
kids that are still home,

00:11:12.139 --> 00:11:13.409
so how do you support both?

00:11:13.409 --> 00:11:15.529
Do you think any of that stuff
is gonna last post-COVID?

00:11:15.529 --> 00:11:16.741
Or do you think we're just gonna go back

00:11:16.741 --> 00:11:18.342
to what we were doing before?

00:11:19.359 --> 00:11:20.419
- That's a good question,

00:11:20.419 --> 00:11:21.929
and something I've been
thinking a lot about.

00:11:21.929 --> 00:11:24.969
I think ideally, this disruption

00:11:24.969 --> 00:11:28.639
should serve as an opportunity
to do things differently

00:11:28.639 --> 00:11:31.069
and hopefully better than we were before,

00:11:31.069 --> 00:11:34.189
that we don't just return
to schools as we knew them.

00:11:34.189 --> 00:11:36.319
For too many kids, school was not a place

00:11:36.319 --> 00:11:37.589
where they felt supported,

00:11:37.589 --> 00:11:40.169
where they felt stimulated and challenged.

00:11:40.169 --> 00:11:42.922
We need that to be the primary focus.

00:11:42.922 --> 00:11:44.709
If you think about it,

00:11:44.709 --> 00:11:46.529
I just did a conversation with the former

00:11:46.529 --> 00:11:49.129
Secretary of Education a few days ago,

00:11:49.129 --> 00:11:52.749
No Child Behind got us
fixated on achievement

00:11:52.749 --> 00:11:54.739
as measured by test scores,

00:11:54.739 --> 00:11:59.629
which then directed teachers
to prepare kids for tests.

00:11:59.629 --> 00:12:04.199
That to me, was such a
disservice to education

00:12:04.199 --> 00:12:07.129
because what we know is that teaching,

00:12:07.129 --> 00:12:09.959
when it's powerful, should be creative,

00:12:09.959 --> 00:12:13.909
it should be stimulating,
it should be engaging,

00:12:13.909 --> 00:12:16.939
it should be meaningful.

00:12:16.939 --> 00:12:18.909
And as a former teacher myself,

00:12:18.909 --> 00:12:20.399
I taught in Providence, Rhode Island,

00:12:20.399 --> 00:12:21.719
when I first started.

00:12:21.719 --> 00:12:24.329
I taught history, and I loved history.

00:12:24.329 --> 00:12:26.259
And I knew then that my job

00:12:26.259 --> 00:12:28.719
was not simply to cover the textbook.

00:12:28.719 --> 00:12:31.179
My job was to make history come to life,

00:12:31.179 --> 00:12:32.949
so that students would appreciate

00:12:32.949 --> 00:12:35.319
and want to learn more history.

00:12:35.319 --> 00:12:37.540
And that's a message
I often tell teachers.

00:12:37.540 --> 00:12:41.059
We know we're doing it
right when kids want more.

00:12:41.059 --> 00:12:43.189
That's the way knowledge works.

00:12:43.189 --> 00:12:46.029
You want more once you start to realize

00:12:46.029 --> 00:12:47.239
the power of knowledge.

00:12:47.239 --> 00:12:48.929
Well that's what we've
got to come back to.

00:12:48.929 --> 00:12:51.503
We've gotta redirect the
attention of our teachers.

00:12:51.503 --> 00:12:54.609
Their jobs should be to get
kids excited about learning,

00:12:54.609 --> 00:12:59.309
to focus on motivation, to
focus on making it meaningful,

00:12:59.309 --> 00:13:01.929
rather than simply covering material

00:13:01.929 --> 00:13:04.619
and assessing to see if kids got it.

00:13:04.619 --> 00:13:05.842
That to me was.

00:13:06.869 --> 00:13:07.819
I mean, I get it.

00:13:07.819 --> 00:13:09.929
We needed the evidence
that kids were learning.

00:13:09.929 --> 00:13:11.942
We just focused on the wrong things.

00:13:12.989 --> 00:13:14.499
- And you feel that post-COVID,

00:13:14.499 --> 00:13:16.269
there might be some
opportunities to rethink this

00:13:16.269 --> 00:13:19.459
because some of the traditional
kind of testing structures

00:13:19.459 --> 00:13:21.939
have, or at least that
they've been paused,

00:13:21.939 --> 00:13:23.589
in some of the state assessments.

00:13:24.489 --> 00:13:28.639
- And also in ESS, Every Student Succeeds,

00:13:28.639 --> 00:13:31.999
replaced No Child Behind,
gives states more flexibility

00:13:31.999 --> 00:13:33.139
to do things differently.

00:13:33.139 --> 00:13:34.529
To do formative assessments

00:13:34.529 --> 00:13:36.609
and performance based assessment.

00:13:36.609 --> 00:13:39.439
So we don't have to allow
the assessment itself

00:13:39.439 --> 00:13:41.969
to be the driving factor.

00:13:41.969 --> 00:13:45.859
But what I see is that in several states,

00:13:45.859 --> 00:13:48.259
I don't see the vision
or the knowledge about

00:13:48.259 --> 00:13:50.449
how to do things differently,
and that worries me.

00:13:50.449 --> 00:13:52.469
If you look at the independent schools,

00:13:52.469 --> 00:13:54.599
they're already doing it differently.

00:13:54.599 --> 00:13:57.719
Many of the affluent public schools are.

00:13:57.719 --> 00:14:00.779
Where we see the problems
is in the schools

00:14:00.779 --> 00:14:02.179
serving low income kids,

00:14:02.179 --> 00:14:05.269
which are over half the
kids in the country today.

00:14:05.269 --> 00:14:09.149
That's where we see the kind
of kill and drill approach

00:14:09.149 --> 00:14:11.372
that I think leaves many kids alienated.

00:14:12.509 --> 00:14:13.552
- No, it makes sense.

00:14:13.552 --> 00:14:15.529
So we have a question here from a teacher,

00:14:15.529 --> 00:14:16.819
Christina Espinosa.

00:14:16.819 --> 00:14:18.189
Good day, Dr. Noguera.

00:14:18.189 --> 00:14:23.019
I am a teacher in the Sweetwater
Union High School District.

00:14:23.019 --> 00:14:25.059
Currently, we are teaching 100% virtually

00:14:25.059 --> 00:14:27.669
and serving students from
extremely diverse backgrounds.

00:14:27.669 --> 00:14:30.179
Since we see students
throughout several zip codes

00:14:30.179 --> 00:14:32.069
from a rather fluent East Chula Vista,

00:14:32.069 --> 00:14:33.619
all the way down to the border,

00:14:33.619 --> 00:14:36.919
we can not have a one size
fits all grading policy.

00:14:36.919 --> 00:14:40.429
How, from your experience,
should we approach grading?

00:14:40.429 --> 00:14:41.262
- Good question.

00:14:41.262 --> 00:14:43.579
And hello to the folks down at Sweetwater.

00:14:43.579 --> 00:14:47.169
They have literally hundreds
of kids, I think each day,

00:14:47.169 --> 00:14:48.699
at least when schools were open,

00:14:48.699 --> 00:14:50.699
were coming across the
border to go to school.

00:14:50.699 --> 00:14:52.399
Some kids getting up
at three in the morning

00:14:52.399 --> 00:14:55.099
to get to school, so I
just wanna acknowledge

00:14:56.059 --> 00:14:59.729
the efforts of those kids and
the educators who serve them.

00:14:59.729 --> 00:15:01.379
I think grading all along,

00:15:01.379 --> 00:15:02.879
and I've been struggling with this

00:15:02.879 --> 00:15:05.032
as a university professor for many years.

00:15:06.739 --> 00:15:10.019
What we forget is the real
learning is not in the grade

00:15:10.019 --> 00:15:13.559
you earn, it's in your
ability to demonstrate

00:15:13.559 --> 00:15:16.629
how much you've learned,
it's the work you produce.

00:15:16.629 --> 00:15:20.369
So we should be using grading
to give feedback to students,

00:15:20.369 --> 00:15:23.479
meaningful feedback on how to improve.

00:15:23.479 --> 00:15:28.479
And we should be treating work
as in an iterative manner,

00:15:28.809 --> 00:15:31.869
that is the real learning
occurs through the revision.

00:15:31.869 --> 00:15:36.729
Of getting it right, or
getting it improving upon

00:15:36.729 --> 00:15:38.009
the finished product.

00:15:38.009 --> 00:15:42.039
And so what always has concerned
about the way we do grading

00:15:42.039 --> 00:15:45.479
in schools, is kids get
fixated on the grade.

00:15:45.479 --> 00:15:47.759
Some kids want the A and
so they'll aim for the A's.

00:15:47.759 --> 00:15:49.619
Some kids just wanna pass.

00:15:49.619 --> 00:15:52.599
And what we should be more
concerned about is the learning.

00:15:52.599 --> 00:15:53.859
How much have they learned?

00:15:53.859 --> 00:15:56.429
What can they share
with us and demonstrate

00:15:56.429 --> 00:15:58.069
is proof of what they've learned?

00:15:58.069 --> 00:15:59.979
That's what we should be looking at.

00:15:59.979 --> 00:16:02.629
And that's what, to the
degree that we need to affix

00:16:02.629 --> 00:16:06.499
a grade at the end, we should
grade evidence of growth,

00:16:06.499 --> 00:16:11.169
evidence of mastery, because
when we don't focus on that,

00:16:11.169 --> 00:16:13.739
what we end up with is
kids who are pushed along

00:16:13.739 --> 00:16:16.719
grade by grade, who
have a weak foundation,

00:16:16.719 --> 00:16:19.789
who can't do very much, end
up in college, can't write,

00:16:19.789 --> 00:16:22.699
can't do research, even
though they passed.

00:16:22.699 --> 00:16:24.342
And that is a huge problem.

00:16:25.229 --> 00:16:26.169
- I couldn't agree more.

00:16:26.169 --> 00:16:28.999
One of the things that
I talk a lot about is

00:16:28.999 --> 00:16:31.132
70% of kids that show
up at community college

00:16:31.132 --> 00:16:32.809
have to take remedial
math, which you know,

00:16:32.809 --> 00:16:35.019
is not a euphemism for
11th or 12th grade math.

00:16:35.019 --> 00:16:38.049
It's pre-algebra, it's
sixth or seventh grade math.

00:16:38.049 --> 00:16:40.439
So the whole system, to
your point, year after year

00:16:40.439 --> 00:16:43.889
kids promoted, they get 20%
wrong on dividing decimals,

00:16:43.889 --> 00:16:45.709
they get 30% wrong on exponents,

00:16:45.709 --> 00:16:47.529
those gaps just keep accumulating.

00:16:47.529 --> 00:16:48.729
At some point, the algebra is hard.

00:16:48.729 --> 00:16:50.149
They start watering it down for them,

00:16:50.149 --> 00:16:52.679
so that they have the
illusion of progress.

00:16:52.679 --> 00:16:53.919
You get to college, and they're saying,

00:16:53.919 --> 00:16:56.429
you're not even ready
to learn algebra yet,

00:16:56.429 --> 00:16:58.589
which is really a ninth
or 10th grade subject.

00:16:58.589 --> 00:17:00.759
And those kids, all of them are capable

00:17:00.759 --> 00:17:01.989
'cause we get letters from them everyday

00:17:01.989 --> 00:17:03.439
when they go back and fill in their gaps,

00:17:03.439 --> 00:17:04.789
they're all capable of it.

00:17:04.789 --> 00:17:06.079
But it's incredibly disheartening,

00:17:06.079 --> 00:17:10.579
and we know that's the
weeder, or the best predictor

00:17:10.579 --> 00:17:12.799
of not succeeding in
college is having to show up

00:17:12.799 --> 00:17:16.679
and having to take a noncredit
bearing remedial courses.

00:17:16.679 --> 00:17:19.379
I mean, with that lens, how do you see,

00:17:19.379 --> 00:17:21.074
and everything that we do at Khan Academy

00:17:21.074 --> 00:17:23.239
is trying to facilitate mastery learning.

00:17:23.239 --> 00:17:25.729
So I love your view there.

00:17:25.729 --> 00:17:28.259
But what's your sense of college?

00:17:28.259 --> 00:17:30.486
There's a question from YouTube (mumbles).

00:17:31.379 --> 00:17:32.899
Do you think college
will change in the future

00:17:32.899 --> 00:17:34.172
because of the pandemic?

00:17:36.169 --> 00:17:37.359
- College is gonna have to change.

00:17:37.359 --> 00:17:39.629
And the main thing that
has to change is the cost.

00:17:39.629 --> 00:17:41.779
It's out of reach for too many people.

00:17:41.779 --> 00:17:44.429
We have too many students
that have to take on debts

00:17:44.429 --> 00:17:49.429
that they'll incur and be
paying back well into adulthood

00:17:50.879 --> 00:17:54.739
because the cost of
college has gone too high.

00:17:54.739 --> 00:17:56.699
So we've gotta figure
out how to reduce costs,

00:17:56.699 --> 00:18:01.009
how to increase access, but
also how to improve teaching.

00:18:01.009 --> 00:18:02.579
So it's very easy.

00:18:02.579 --> 00:18:05.449
We often, in the
conversations about teaching

00:18:05.449 --> 00:18:07.899
and the critiques, we focus on K-12,

00:18:07.899 --> 00:18:10.059
but I would say some of
the worst teaching I see

00:18:10.059 --> 00:18:11.439
is at the university level,

00:18:11.439 --> 00:18:15.489
where the primary mode of
teaching is still lecture.

00:18:15.489 --> 00:18:20.489
And lecture, even if you're
a very good lecturer,

00:18:20.769 --> 00:18:22.449
is not the most effective way

00:18:22.449 --> 00:18:24.319
for students to learn something.

00:18:24.319 --> 00:18:28.079
We need to know real,
get the evidence again.

00:18:28.079 --> 00:18:31.439
Evidence-based teaching is
a much more interactive.

00:18:31.439 --> 00:18:32.659
Let me just use the one example,

00:18:32.659 --> 00:18:34.899
going back to math and
algebra that you gave.

00:18:34.899 --> 00:18:39.039
When I was in eighth grade, I
took algebra with my teacher,

00:18:39.039 --> 00:18:41.249
Ms. Harris, in New York City.

00:18:41.249 --> 00:18:43.779
And Ms. Harris started class by saying,

00:18:43.779 --> 00:18:44.949
you kids are lucky

00:18:44.949 --> 00:18:47.329
'cause I'm the best teacher in the school.

00:18:47.329 --> 00:18:50.579
She said, tell me who
out there in this class

00:18:50.579 --> 00:18:51.879
struggles in math?

00:18:51.879 --> 00:18:54.209
And very sheepishly I raised my hand

00:18:54.209 --> 00:18:56.329
because I was fine in arithmetic,

00:18:56.329 --> 00:19:00.359
but algebra was throwing me for a loop.

00:19:00.359 --> 00:19:02.819
So she said, you kids
are gonna sit in front.

00:19:02.819 --> 00:19:06.679
'Cause I'm going to check
on you throughout the day

00:19:06.679 --> 00:19:08.569
and everyday to make sure you with me.

00:19:08.569 --> 00:19:11.289
'Cause she understood
that if you didn't get it

00:19:11.289 --> 00:19:14.199
in September, you certainly
wouldn't have it by June.

00:19:14.199 --> 00:19:15.989
She was constantly looking at our work,

00:19:15.989 --> 00:19:17.639
she wanted proof that we got it.

00:19:17.639 --> 00:19:21.429
She was inviting our
questions, not to embarrass us,

00:19:21.429 --> 00:19:23.819
but make sure that we were with her.

00:19:23.819 --> 00:19:25.349
It was interactive.

00:19:25.349 --> 00:19:27.999
And because of that, I
came out of that course

00:19:27.999 --> 00:19:30.289
much more confident about my knowledge

00:19:30.289 --> 00:19:31.919
and understanding of math.

00:19:31.919 --> 00:19:36.099
When we teach that way, in
that kind of interactive way,

00:19:36.099 --> 00:19:38.969
when we recognize that teaching
and learning are connected,

00:19:38.969 --> 00:19:41.659
and Khan Academy does
this better than I think

00:19:41.659 --> 00:19:46.239
most people, then what learning becomes

00:19:46.239 --> 00:19:51.239
an exercise in acquiring and
becoming and getting ownership

00:19:51.619 --> 00:19:54.919
of the information, material, the skills,

00:19:54.919 --> 00:19:58.029
instead of a passive experience
of sitting through lessons.

00:19:58.029 --> 00:20:00.369
And that I think, is the
biggest change at both

00:20:00.369 --> 00:20:02.988
the university and at the K-12 level

00:20:02.988 --> 00:20:05.379
that we need to bring about.

00:20:05.379 --> 00:20:07.549
- It's fascinating 'cause
these days during COVID,

00:20:07.549 --> 00:20:09.289
with everyone learning
on video conference,

00:20:09.289 --> 00:20:10.719
everyone's like how do
we make it engaging?

00:20:10.719 --> 00:20:14.129
'Cause kids are going on
different window, video windows,

00:20:14.129 --> 00:20:16.959
and everyone's saying that
you gotta ask them questions,

00:20:16.959 --> 00:20:18.099
you gotta be on top of them,

00:20:18.099 --> 00:20:19.699
you gotta pull them out of the screen,

00:20:19.699 --> 00:20:20.899
put them into breakouts.

00:20:20.899 --> 00:20:23.229
And it's always like by the way,

00:20:23.229 --> 00:20:27.019
that's also a good idea when
you're in a physical classroom.

00:20:27.019 --> 00:20:28.949
Your point about mastery learning,

00:20:28.949 --> 00:20:30.229
it sounds like you had a great teacher,

00:20:30.229 --> 00:20:33.969
who intuitively got that
you can't let these kids

00:20:33.969 --> 00:20:38.969
have the gaps because it becomes
a self-fulfilling prophecy.

00:20:39.063 --> 00:20:40.809
If you don't know how to dribble,

00:20:40.809 --> 00:20:42.669
you can practice free throws all day.

00:20:42.669 --> 00:20:45.259
You're not gonna be a
great basketball player.

00:20:45.259 --> 00:20:49.589
But somehow the system, and
I don't blame the system,

00:20:49.589 --> 00:20:52.159
because it's the best
way we knew how to do it

00:20:52.159 --> 00:20:54.092
200 years ago, but there's now.

00:20:56.739 --> 00:20:58.259
To your point, your
teacher was able to do it

00:20:58.259 --> 00:21:00.059
with pretty low tech, so
maybe we could have done it

00:21:00.059 --> 00:21:02.379
a little bit different awhile ago.

00:21:02.379 --> 00:21:03.779
- She said I'm the best teacher.

00:21:03.779 --> 00:21:05.059
And best teacher to her,

00:21:05.059 --> 00:21:07.449
meant not that she taught the best kids,

00:21:07.449 --> 00:21:10.199
best teacher meant she could
teach all kinds of kids.

00:21:10.199 --> 00:21:12.099
See, and that's also a shift

00:21:12.099 --> 00:21:13.949
in the way we think about teaching.

00:21:13.949 --> 00:21:16.309
There are a lot of people out
there who think they're good,

00:21:16.309 --> 00:21:19.102
but they're only good with
kids who don't need help.

00:21:20.059 --> 00:21:21.529
They only wanna teach honors.

00:21:21.529 --> 00:21:23.409
They only wanna teach AP.

00:21:23.409 --> 00:21:25.459
They don't wanna teach kids who struggle.

00:21:25.459 --> 00:21:26.849
And that's part of the problem,

00:21:26.849 --> 00:21:30.739
that real teaching is about
helping to make it accessible,

00:21:30.739 --> 00:21:34.249
make it, get it, help kids to get it.

00:21:34.249 --> 00:21:37.049
So I think the shift needs
to occur on both ends,

00:21:37.049 --> 00:21:41.169
in how we deliver, but also
how we engage students.

00:21:41.169 --> 00:21:42.829
If I can just use one analogy.

00:21:42.829 --> 00:21:45.309
If you watch kids when
they're playing a video game,

00:21:45.309 --> 00:21:49.009
a new game, if they need
help, they don't get it,

00:21:49.009 --> 00:21:51.549
they go on YouTube, they call a friend,

00:21:51.549 --> 00:21:53.459
that's part of learning.

00:21:53.459 --> 00:21:54.839
Then they're making mistakes

00:21:54.839 --> 00:21:56.099
because that's part of learning, too.

00:21:56.099 --> 00:21:57.879
You learn through your mistakes.

00:21:57.879 --> 00:21:59.539
And then they advance.

00:21:59.539 --> 00:22:02.449
They advance because they
get better and better.

00:22:02.449 --> 00:22:04.309
And then they master the game.

00:22:04.309 --> 00:22:06.479
That's the way learning occurs for kids.

00:22:06.479 --> 00:22:09.319
And that's what we should be
replicating in our classrooms.

00:22:09.319 --> 00:22:10.302
A version of that.

00:22:11.399 --> 00:22:12.809
- I couldn't agree more.

00:22:12.809 --> 00:22:16.569
That's what we aspire
exactly to help facilitate.

00:22:16.569 --> 00:22:19.549
It was a good question here
from YouTube, Niche786.

00:22:19.549 --> 00:22:21.229
You're widely known for
your work on equity,

00:22:21.229 --> 00:22:23.569
race, and poverty issues in education.

00:22:23.569 --> 00:22:25.129
How do you see social emotional learning

00:22:25.129 --> 00:22:26.579
fitting into those areas?

00:22:26.579 --> 00:22:28.049
If schools aren't addressing racism,

00:22:28.049 --> 00:22:30.939
they aren't fully addressing
trauma, in quotes.

00:22:30.939 --> 00:22:32.619
How can schools better align their work

00:22:32.619 --> 00:22:34.182
in these related areas?

00:22:35.239 --> 00:22:38.669
- So, one of the things we
know, all teachers know this,

00:22:38.669 --> 00:22:40.769
kids learn through relationships.

00:22:40.769 --> 00:22:43.829
Kids will learn from teachers
they know care about them,

00:22:43.829 --> 00:22:45.449
who are invested in them.

00:22:45.449 --> 00:22:47.909
And caring doesn't mean we
have to be best buddies.

00:22:47.909 --> 00:22:50.059
My teacher, Ms. Harris,
wasn't my best buddy.

00:22:50.059 --> 00:22:51.789
She let me know that
if you're in my class,

00:22:51.789 --> 00:22:53.249
you're gonna learn, you're gonna work.

00:22:53.249 --> 00:22:55.929
She was that warm demander.

00:22:55.929 --> 00:22:57.869
Racism gets in the way of that

00:22:57.869 --> 00:23:00.829
because it often results
in lowered expectations.

00:23:00.829 --> 00:23:03.619
It results in strained relationships.

00:23:03.619 --> 00:23:06.879
And sometimes it results
in teachers who don't see

00:23:06.879 --> 00:23:10.159
themselves as being
invested in the learner,

00:23:10.159 --> 00:23:12.318
taking responsibility for whether or not

00:23:12.318 --> 00:23:14.789
their students are actually learning.

00:23:14.789 --> 00:23:19.789
So I think it's important that
we have an equity mindset,

00:23:21.409 --> 00:23:25.169
and we recognize that
professional educators

00:23:25.169 --> 00:23:28.219
have a moral and
professional responsibility

00:23:28.219 --> 00:23:30.319
to educate all their students.

00:23:30.319 --> 00:23:33.199
And if they can't, they
need to let someone know,

00:23:33.199 --> 00:23:35.539
I've got a kid in my
class who's not learning,

00:23:35.539 --> 00:23:37.079
and they need to call for help.

00:23:37.079 --> 00:23:39.059
To do anything less, in my opinion,

00:23:39.059 --> 00:23:40.609
is a form of malpractice.

00:23:40.609 --> 00:23:44.339
And teachers should not be complicit

00:23:44.339 --> 00:23:47.149
in allowing kids not to learn.

00:23:47.149 --> 00:23:51.129
When you see kids end up
in high school or college

00:23:51.129 --> 00:23:53.159
who haven't learned basic math,

00:23:53.159 --> 00:23:55.439
that means there were
educators all along the way

00:23:55.439 --> 00:23:57.142
who saw it, and did nothing.

00:23:58.429 --> 00:24:00.269
- And what advice would you,

00:24:00.269 --> 00:24:01.999
I think everyone agrees intellectually,

00:24:01.999 --> 00:24:04.415
but if you can imagine
you're a ninth grade teacher,

00:24:04.415 --> 00:24:07.049
you have five kids in your class that are

00:24:07.049 --> 00:24:09.139
barely struggling, you kind
of water it down for them,

00:24:09.139 --> 00:24:11.549
they barely scratch by on a C.

00:24:11.549 --> 00:24:14.249
They're gonna be those kids
who have to take remedial math,

00:24:14.249 --> 00:24:17.709
if they even get through high
school and get to college.

00:24:17.709 --> 00:24:20.259
But that teacher's feeling
like, hey, there's 30 kids here.

00:24:20.259 --> 00:24:21.449
I gotta do what I can.

00:24:21.449 --> 00:24:22.639
I told the administration,

00:24:22.639 --> 00:24:25.529
the administration's kind of saying, look,

00:24:25.529 --> 00:24:26.539
that's the way life is.

00:24:26.539 --> 00:24:30.849
Like how does someone, from
a teacher's point of view,

00:24:30.849 --> 00:24:33.529
or does it have to happen
at the systemic level?

00:24:33.529 --> 00:24:34.584
How do we make sure that

00:24:34.584 --> 00:24:37.809
there are ways to support those kids?

00:24:37.809 --> 00:24:41.439
- Truthfully it's hard for
a teacher by themselves

00:24:41.439 --> 00:24:44.009
to effect system change.

00:24:44.009 --> 00:24:46.319
They can change what's
happening in their classroom.

00:24:46.319 --> 00:24:48.739
They can create the
climate in their classroom,

00:24:48.739 --> 00:24:51.529
where kids feel supported and engaged.

00:24:51.529 --> 00:24:56.529
And the truth is that when
the classroom door closes,

00:24:56.559 --> 00:24:58.499
the teachers have a lot of power,

00:24:58.499 --> 00:25:01.299
at least one way in a
physical space together.

00:25:01.299 --> 00:25:04.249
But to bring about real system
change, you need leaders,

00:25:04.249 --> 00:25:05.789
principals, superintendents,

00:25:05.789 --> 00:25:08.589
who understand that
we've gotta become better

00:25:08.589 --> 00:25:12.649
at intervening early with
kids, better at providing,

00:25:12.649 --> 00:25:15.339
some kids as you know Sal, need more time

00:25:15.339 --> 00:25:17.229
than the traditional school day allows.

00:25:17.229 --> 00:25:19.689
So we need to find ways to
create more time, as well,

00:25:19.689 --> 00:25:22.033
for the kids on subjects
that take more time

00:25:22.033 --> 00:25:23.372
for them to learn.

00:25:24.349 --> 00:25:25.709
- Yeah, it's always blown my mind

00:25:25.709 --> 00:25:27.419
that slow is equated with dumb,

00:25:27.419 --> 00:25:29.929
when oftentimes the kids that
just need a little more time,

00:25:29.929 --> 00:25:31.979
they're really trying to internalize it.

00:25:31.979 --> 00:25:34.033
And if they have a strong
foundation, they're gonna,

00:25:34.033 --> 00:25:36.559
we see that at Khan Academy
all the time in our own data.

00:25:36.559 --> 00:25:38.315
So I couldn't agree with you more.

00:25:38.315 --> 00:25:40.729
That equation is really flawed,

00:25:40.729 --> 00:25:42.629
and it makes kids even
think that they're not smart

00:25:42.629 --> 00:25:43.699
when they're fully capable.

00:25:43.699 --> 00:25:45.319
- One of the what are the attributes

00:25:45.319 --> 00:25:48.269
that kids most frequently
cite as being valuable to them

00:25:48.269 --> 00:25:50.412
from a teacher, is patience.

00:25:51.479 --> 00:25:55.009
Teachers who are patient
and giving them the chance

00:25:55.009 --> 00:25:58.712
and teachers who are organized
in how they put them.

00:26:00.289 --> 00:26:01.859
- No, I remember when I
was tutoring my cousins,

00:26:01.859 --> 00:26:03.219
I mean, I tell the story all the time,

00:26:03.219 --> 00:26:05.829
that when I first made some
of those videos for them,

00:26:05.829 --> 00:26:08.219
they told me famously that
they liked me better on YouTube

00:26:08.219 --> 00:26:09.259
than in person.

00:26:09.259 --> 00:26:11.619
And I think part of it,

00:26:11.619 --> 00:26:13.549
and I always try to parse
what they were saying,

00:26:13.549 --> 00:26:15.679
'cause they weren't saying they
really appreciated having me

00:26:15.679 --> 00:26:18.999
as like a real person
who is invested in them.

00:26:18.999 --> 00:26:21.839
But I think the video comment
was really about the patience

00:26:21.839 --> 00:26:24.399
that they didn't feel
the pressure to learn it

00:26:24.399 --> 00:26:25.589
right in that three minutes,

00:26:25.589 --> 00:26:27.139
that they could come
back to it, et cetera.

00:26:27.139 --> 00:26:27.972
They had multiple opportunities.

00:26:27.972 --> 00:26:31.999
- They could pause the video
and then pick it up again,

00:26:31.999 --> 00:26:34.059
that's 'cause again, that's how we learn.

00:26:34.059 --> 00:26:36.322
And we don't all learn at the same pace.

00:26:37.749 --> 00:26:39.079
- No, couldn't agree more.

00:26:39.079 --> 00:26:40.084
So the time we have left,

00:26:40.084 --> 00:26:42.979
we have teachers watching
this, we have students,

00:26:42.979 --> 00:26:45.279
we have a lot of parents,
and obviously everyone's got

00:26:45.279 --> 00:26:47.299
a certain level of anxiety these days

00:26:47.299 --> 00:26:49.699
above and beyond what they
normally would have had.

00:26:49.699 --> 00:26:52.539
What advice do you have about
where education is going?

00:26:52.539 --> 00:26:54.079
How people should navigate it?

00:26:54.079 --> 00:26:55.092
Any big takeaways?

00:26:56.089 --> 00:26:59.589
- Yeah, I'd say right
now we have to use again,

00:26:59.589 --> 00:27:01.049
use this disruption to
start thinking about

00:27:01.049 --> 00:27:02.999
what kind of schools do we wanna create?

00:27:02.999 --> 00:27:05.129
What kind of classrooms
do we wanna create?

00:27:05.129 --> 00:27:08.879
Start visualize what the
end should look like.

00:27:08.879 --> 00:27:11.939
Imagine schools with kids who
are excited about learning,

00:27:11.939 --> 00:27:13.599
where they were happy to come each day.

00:27:13.599 --> 00:27:16.029
What would it take to create
those kinds of schools?

00:27:16.029 --> 00:27:17.699
And let's start planning for that.

00:27:17.699 --> 00:27:21.259
Let's start planning schools
where relationships are valued,

00:27:21.259 --> 00:27:24.179
where we are thinking holistically
about the needs of kids,

00:27:24.179 --> 00:27:27.229
the academic, the social,
the emotional needs of kids,

00:27:27.229 --> 00:27:28.569
'cause they're all integrated.

00:27:28.569 --> 00:27:31.189
And let's start thinking
about how to really organize

00:27:31.189 --> 00:27:34.179
schools to meet the needs of our students.

00:27:34.179 --> 00:27:36.099
If we did that, then I
think we would end up

00:27:36.099 --> 00:27:38.349
with very different
schools than we have now.

00:27:39.589 --> 00:27:40.839
- No, couldn't agree more.

00:27:40.839 --> 00:27:42.159
And what I always stress is,

00:27:42.159 --> 00:27:43.539
people view me as a technologist,

00:27:43.539 --> 00:27:45.209
but I don't view myself that way.

00:27:45.209 --> 00:27:48.779
I always say never use
technology for technology's sake.

00:27:48.779 --> 00:27:50.679
Always think about what is the goal.

00:27:50.679 --> 00:27:54.689
And 99% of the time, the
best possible resource

00:27:54.689 --> 00:27:57.929
is an amazing in-person
teacher for your child.

00:27:57.929 --> 00:28:00.819
And if technology can help
unlock what that teacher can do,

00:28:00.819 --> 00:28:03.559
personalize more, fill in
gaps, mastery learning,

00:28:03.559 --> 00:28:04.429
then that's all good.

00:28:04.429 --> 00:28:06.459
But technology has to be
in service to the humans

00:28:06.459 --> 00:28:08.269
and in service of those goals.

00:28:08.269 --> 00:28:09.359
- Absolutely, I agree.

00:28:09.359 --> 00:28:10.459
I couldn't agree more.

00:28:11.579 --> 00:28:14.769
- Well, Dean Noguera, thank
you so much for joining.

00:28:14.769 --> 00:28:15.679
This is one of those conversations

00:28:15.679 --> 00:28:17.699
I think we could continue
for another several hours.

00:28:17.699 --> 00:28:20.299
But I'll let you go for now.

00:28:20.299 --> 00:28:21.479
But thank you so much for joining.

00:28:21.479 --> 00:28:22.619
This was really valuable.

00:28:22.619 --> 00:28:25.479
- And Sal, I wanna thank you
